For Aggregation, both constructor injection and setter injection work, but in interviews and real projects,
constructor injection is usually preferred.

1. Constructor Injection (Preferred ✅)

class Department {

    private Teacher teacher;

    Department(Teacher teacher) {
        this.teacher = teacher;
    }
}

Usage:

Teacher t = new Teacher("Rahul");

Department d = new Department(t);

Advantages:

✅ Object is fully initialized when created.

✅ No chance of forgetting to set the Teacher.

✅ More secure and reliable.

✅ Commonly used in Spring Framework (Dependency Injection).

------------------------------------------------------------------------------

2. Setter Injection

class Department {

    private Teacher teacher;

    void setTeacher(Teacher teacher) {
        this.teacher = teacher;
    }
}

Usage:

Teacher t = new Teacher("Rahul");

Department d = new Department();

d.setTeacher(t);

Advantages:

✅ Can change Teacher later.

✅ Useful when dependency is optional.

Disadvantage:

Department d = new Department();

d.show();

If teacher was never set:

NullPointerException

can occur.


-> Interview Answer:

For Aggregation, constructor injection is generally preferred because it ensures the required object is provided during object creation
and prevents incomplete initialization. Setter injection is useful when the dependency is optional or needs to be changed later.